  <data name="HomePage_MergeTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Live Photo Merge</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_MergeDesc.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Combine images + videos into single-file Live Photos with Google Micro Video (v1) / Google Motion Photo (v2) / OPPO / vivo / Samsung / HUAWEI protocols, viewable on Windows and Android. Supports images (JPG/JPEG, HEIC/HEIF) + videos (MOV/MP4).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_MergeUsage.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Place the prepared (photos JPG/HEIC + MOV/MP4 video) in the same folder, and click "Start Combining".</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_SplitTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Live Photo Split</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_SplitDesc.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The existing single-file live photos are losslessly unpacked to extract the original still images and dynamic videos.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_SplitUsage.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Select the folder of photos you want to split, and click "Start Splitting".</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Live Photo Display Repair</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairSubtitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Fix rotation, stretching and thumbnail issues of Apple Live Photos on Windows</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTutorial_Step1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>What Does It Fix?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTutorial_Step1_Desc.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>After exporting iPhone Live Photos, common display issues include:

1. Stretched Thumbnail with Extra Embedded Data (pre‑iOS 17.3):
Apple embedded a low-resolution thumbnail for compatibility, but it carried an orientation tag that Windows misinterpreted as landscape, causing the thumbnail to be stretched or squished.
iOS 17.3 and later no longer embed this data.
Fixed by lossless jpegtran rotation and stripping the leftover embedded thumbnail.

2. Front camera Live Photo video portion may rotate:
The iPhone front camera stores portrait pixels horizontally, relying on an orientation tag for correct display — but Windows ignores it. Corrected via FFmpeg re-encoding.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTutorial_Step2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scan &amp; Review Diagnosis</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTutorial_Step2_Desc.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Click "Browse" to select a photo folder, then click "Scan Directory" to start the automatic analysis.

Once scanning is complete, the stats panel shows an overview:
· Total Photos / Live Photos — folder summary
· Normal — no repair needed, photo is perfectly healthy
· Abnormal — rotation, stretching, or extra thumbnails detected; repair needed
· Lone Img / Lone Vid — files not paired as Live Photos

The "Diagnosis" column on the right shows the specific issue for each photo. Click any abnormal entry to view detailed descriptions. Use the filters at the top to quickly find files by type or repair status.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTutorial_Step3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose Output Mode &amp; Start Repair</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTutorial_Step3_Desc.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Once confirmed, select an output mode:

· Output to Directory — Repaired files are saved to a separate folder, keeping originals untouched (recommended for first use)
· Replace Original — Repairs files in place; please back up first

Click "Start Repair" and the app will handle everything automatically:
· JPEG → jpegtran lossless rotation + strip extra thumbnails
· HEIC → correct erroneous Orientation tags
· Video → FFmpeg re-encode to remove rotation matrix

Open the output folder when done — your photos are back to normal ~</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_RepairTutorial_Reassurance.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Photos not taken with an iPhone generally don't need repair — scanning or repairing won't affect normal photos in any way. To scan only iPhone Live Photos, enable "Strict Live Photo Scanning".</value>
  </data>
